---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { concepts, conceptById } from '../data/concepts';
import { domains, regions } from '../data/domains';
import { dependenciesOf, getGraph, topologicalOrder } from '../lib/graph/graph';
import { hrefFor } from '../lib/lesson-context';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const g = getGraph();
const lessons = await getCollection('lessons');
const written = new Set(lessons.map((l) => l.id));
const LEVEL = { beginner: 'Beginner', intermediate: 'Intermediate', seasoned: 'Seasoned' } as const;
const nConcepts = concepts.filter((c) => c.kind === 'concept' || c.kind === 'integration').length;
const nOptional = concepts.filter((c) => c.optional).length;
const nCapstones = concepts.filter((c) => c.kind === 'capstone').length;
const nLessons = written.size;

const byRegion = regions.map((r) => ({
  r,
  domains: domains
    .filter((d) => d.region === r.id)
    .map((d) => ({ d, items: topologicalOrder(g, concepts.filter((c) => c.domain === d.id).map((c) => c.id)).map((id) => conceptById[id]) })),
}));
---
<BaseLayout title="Curriculum" section="curriculum" description="The complete curriculum as text: every domain, concept, lesson and prerequisite.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Curriculum outline</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
      <h2 id="how-title">How the curriculum is structured</h2>
      <p>
        The curriculum is a network, not a list. {nConcepts} <strong>concepts</strong> are grouped into {domains.length} <strong>domains</strong>, and the
        domains into {regions.length} <strong>regions</strong>: the language foundations, data and program structure, the modelling destinations and
        scientific inference. A concept is one teachable idea, such as integer division or the Gillespie algorithm. Each one belongs
        to a domain, is taught in a lesson (the {nLessons} lessons usually teach several related concepts) and lists the concepts it needs first.
      </p>
      <dl class="how-terms">
        <dt>Levels</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            <span class="lvl lvl-beginner">Beginner</span> concepts need no earlier C++. <span class="lvl lvl-intermediate">Intermediate</span> concepts
            assume you can already read and write ordinary programs and want to build scientific simulations.
            <span class="lvl lvl-seasoned">Seasoned</span> concepts are for people who know C++ or modelling and want more advanced simulation techniques.
            On the <a href={url('/start/')}>start page</a> you choose a level, and routes skip the concepts below it. Nothing is locked: every lesson is open to everyone.
          </p>
        </dd>
        <dt>Prerequisites</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>Each concept lists what it needs. Lessons follow that order inside a domain, and a route to a goal is the chain of prerequisites that ends at it.</p>
        </dd>
        <dt>Destinations</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            A destination is a kind of model you can build once you have the concepts behind it: algorithms and discrete-time models, ordinary differential
            equations, stochastic models, and individual-based models. Each has a <a href={url('/models/')}>page of its own</a> with a laboratory, a model
            assembly view that links every term of the mathematics to a line of C++, and its goals. Scientific inference is the last region: it uses all four
            destinations to design experiments, analyse output and state what a model can and cannot support.
          </p>
        </dd>
        <dt>Capstones</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            A capstone is a project that finishes a destination: you use everything on the way there to answer one biological question with a model you
            build yourself. There are {nCapstones}: two for algorithms and one each for the other destinations and for inference. Each states the question, the model and its equations, a
            series of milestones, the checks that show the code is right, and what to hand in. No solutions are published, so the checks are how you judge your own
            work. See the <a href={url('/capstones/')}>list of capstones</a>.
          </p>
        </dd>
        <dt>Integration concepts</dt>
        <dd><p>One concept, a complete procedural simulation, pulls the foundations together into a single program before the modelling destinations begin.</p></dd>
        <dt>Optional branches</dt>
        <dd><p>{nOptional} concepts are marked optional. They go deeper than the main route needs, and you can skip them without losing the thread.</p></dd>
        <dt>Practice</dt>
        <dd>
          <p>
            Lessons contain worked examples that are compiled and run, step-through traces, knowledge checks and practice tasks. The laboratories are simulations that run in
            your browser. They are not C++: the C++ programs are compiled and checked separately against them.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>The full list follows, region by region, with the level and the prerequisites of every concept.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="split">
      <div class="outline">
        {byRegion.map(({ r, domains: ds }) => (
          <section class="outline-region" id={r.id} aria-labelledby={`h-${r.id}`}>
            <div class="sec-head"><h2 id={`h-${r.id}`}>{r.title}</h2></div>
            <p class="outline-summary">{r.summary}</p>
            {ds.map(({ d, items }) => (
              <section class="outline-domain" aria-labelledby={`h-${d.id}`}>
                <div class="domain-head">
                  <h3 id={`h-${d.id}`}>
                    {d.title}
                    {!d.modules.length && <span class="domain-note"> (original to this site)</span>}
                  </h3>
                  <a href={url(`/atlas/?domain=${d.id}`)}>Show in the atlas</a>
                </div>
                <ul class="item-list">
                  {items.map((c) => {
                    const deps = dependenciesOf(g, c.id).map((id) => conceptById[id]);
                    const available = c.kind === 'landmark' || c.kind === 'capstone' || (c.lesson && written.has(c.lesson));
                    return (
                      <li class="item">
                        <h4 class="item-title">
                          {available ? <a href={hrefFor(c)}>{c.title}</a> : c.title}
                          <span class={c.kind === 'concept' ? `lvl lvl-${c.level}` : 'tag'}>{c.kind === 'concept' ? LEVEL[c.level] : c.kind === 'landmark' ? 'Destination' : c.kind === 'capstone' ? 'Capstone' : 'Integration'}</span>
                          {c.optional && <span class="tag">Optional</span>}
                          {!available && <span class="tag">In preparation</span>}
                        </h4>
                        <p class="item-text">{c.summary}</p>
                        <p class="item-meta">
                          {deps.length > 0 ? <>Needs: {deps.map((dp, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={hrefFor(dp)}>{dp.title}</a></>))}</> : 'Starting point'}
                        </p>
                      </li>
                    );
                  })}
                </ul>
              </section>
            ))}
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="side" aria-label="Contents">
        <p class="side-title">Regions</p>
        <ul>
          {byRegion.map(({ r }) => <li><a href={`#${r.id}`}>{r.title}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="side-title">Elsewhere</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={url('/atlas/')}>The atlas, as a map</a></li>
          <li><a href={url('/models/')}>Destinations and goals</a></li>
          <li><a href={url('/start/')}>Find your route</a></li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .how { max-width: 68ch; margin: 8px 0 48px; }
  .how h2 { padding-bottom: 6px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--frame); }
  .how-terms { margin: 1.4em 0; }
  .how-terms dt { font-weight: 700; margin-top: 1em; }
  .how-terms dd { margin: 0.2em 0 0; }
  .how-terms dd p { margin: 0; }
  .how-terms .lvl { font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .outline-region { scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-h) + 16px); }
  .outline-region + .outline-region { margin-top: 64px; }
  .outline-summary { color: var(--v-secondary); max-width: 70ch; }
  .outline-domain { margin-top: 36px; }
  .domain-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 4px 20px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .domain-head h3 { margin: 0; }
  .domain-head a { font-size: var(--t-small); }
  .domain-note { font-size: var(--t-small); font-weight: 400; color: var(--v-muted); }
  .outline .item { padding: 16px 0; }
  .outline .item:first-child { padding-top: 12px; }
  .outline .item-title { font-size: 1.15rem; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 4px 10px; }
  .outline .item-title .tag { font-weight: 400; }
  .outline .item-text { margin-top: 2px; font-size: 1rem; }
  .outline .item-meta a { color: var(--v-secondary); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
  .outline .item-meta a:hover { color: var(--accent-strong); }
</style>
